1
00:00:00,260 --> 00:00:02,140
This is an 80-year life.

2
00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:04,460
Each little square is one week.

3
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:07,480
All of it adds up to about 4,000 weeks,

4
00:00:07,780 --> 00:00:10,080
and all of them fit on this one page.

5
00:00:11,020 --> 00:00:13,120
4,000 might seem like a big number...

6
00:00:13,460 --> 00:00:15,300
until you compare it to something else:

7
00:00:15,780 --> 00:00:18,560
At a normal pace of 120 steps a minute,

8
00:00:18,720 --> 00:00:20,600
your whole life would come down to

9
00:00:20,900 --> 00:00:22,640
a 35-minute walk!

10
00:00:23,640 --> 00:00:24,860
The first 3 years,

11
00:00:24,940 --> 00:00:26,400
which you don't remember at all.

12
00:00:26,760 --> 00:00:28,940
You spend about 40 of those years at work.

13
00:00:29,340 --> 00:00:31,560
But all these rows don't feel the same:

14
00:00:32,280 --> 00:00:33,100
When you're 10,

15
00:00:33,300 --> 00:00:35,840
one year is 10% of your whole life.

16
00:00:36,160 --> 00:00:38,280
But at 50, it's only 2%.

17
00:00:38,760 --> 00:00:40,600
Maybe that's why childhood summers

18
00:00:40,620 --> 00:00:41,580
felt endless...

19
00:00:41,960 --> 00:00:44,040
But now the years fly by like lightning.

20
00:00:45,040 --> 00:00:47,720
In 2015, a writer named Tim Urban

21
00:00:47,740 --> 00:00:49,240
looked at his own life this way,

22
00:00:49,340 --> 00:00:51,040
and landed on something startling:

23
00:00:52,460 --> 00:00:54,400
What's running out isn't just weeks.

24
00:00:54,780 --> 00:00:56,720
It's times, and chances.

25
00:00:57,220 --> 00:00:59,880
If you're 30 now and go to the sea once a year,

26
00:01:00,020 --> 00:01:02,760
you have about 50 more chances to go, in total.

27
00:01:03,500 --> 00:01:04,920
If you read one book a month,

28
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:07,119
you can read 600 more at most;

29
00:01:07,400 --> 00:01:09,720
and that's in a world with nearly 130

30
00:01:09,800 --> 00:01:11,140
million printed books...

31
00:01:11,560 --> 00:01:12,420
To read them all,

32
00:01:12,640 --> 00:01:15,360
you'd need more than 200,000 lifetimes

33
00:01:15,380 --> 00:01:16,120
like your own!

34
00:01:17,580 --> 00:01:19,080
When it comes to relationships with

35
00:01:19,100 --> 00:01:21,000
people, it's exactly the same:

36
00:01:21,380 --> 00:01:22,380
According to the data,

37
00:01:22,560 --> 00:01:24,900
18-year-olds spend about 2 hours a day

38
00:01:24,920 --> 00:01:25,700
with their friends;

39
00:01:25,980 --> 00:01:28,660
but by 40, that drops to half an hour a day.

40
00:01:29,080 --> 00:01:31,840
In return, the time you spend alone doubles:

41
00:01:32,160 --> 00:01:34,080
from 4 hours a day to 8.

42
00:01:35,580 --> 00:01:37,260
Tim Urban did the same math for his

43
00:01:37,280 --> 00:01:38,100
parents, too:

44
00:01:38,500 --> 00:01:41,240
Until 18, he saw them almost every day,

45
00:01:41,540 --> 00:01:42,320
but after that,

46
00:01:42,440 --> 00:01:44,360
it came to maybe 10 days a year.

47
00:01:45,300 --> 00:01:47,020
93% of all the time

48
00:01:47,040 --> 00:01:49,160
he was ever going to spend with his parents

49
00:01:49,320 --> 00:01:50,620
was already used up!

50
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:54,720
We almost never realize when it's the last time:

51
00:01:55,040 --> 00:01:56,660
the last time someone hugged you and

52
00:01:56,680 --> 00:01:57,480
lifted you up...

53
00:01:57,760 --> 00:01:59,360
the last afternoon you played in the

54
00:01:59,380 --> 00:02:00,940
street with your childhood friends...

55
00:02:01,620 --> 00:02:03,660
and that this was the last time.

56
00:02:05,120 --> 00:02:07,540
Nearly 2,000 years ago, Seneca wrote:

57
00:02:07,560 --> 00:02:09,800
We don't have too little time;

58
00:02:10,100 --> 00:02:11,840
we waste a great deal of it.

59
00:02:12,960 --> 00:02:15,460
4,000 weeks may not be enough for everything,

60
00:02:15,760 --> 00:02:18,459
but for the things you truly choose, they are.

61
00:02:19,480 --> 00:02:21,400
So maybe the real question isn't

62
00:02:21,420 --> 00:02:22,960
how many weeks you have left;

63
00:02:23,640 --> 00:02:25,600
Who are you going to spend these weeks with?
